# Candidate toolchain and native comparison qualification

## Historical native PNPM comparison (retained source implementation)

The earlier BCF c274c8d49e2c7aee667c578f6c71bdc3266104f1 root used
PNPM11.28.5 with an independently frozen workspace installation, package-owned
builds, native packing and a Guix output. Its former `justfile` blob was
5a8b35f93efbc4cafe89adad370da8196627b40f. The current BCF source has
retired that root workspace, its lock, and the old common-software fabrication
recipes. The retained `runNativeDeclaredReceiving` is **historical comparison
capability**, not an executable current-enterprise build path simply because
it is present on Guix main.

The current software provider is the original Code-owned registry closure:
`@red-cup-engineering/npm-workspace-publication-service/source-registry-closure`
selects and packs exact source-owned releases, and the existing committed-lock
consumer installs its real packages. LAGOON accepts that installed portable
runtime as `GUIX_DEPLOYMENT_SOURCE`, builds the Guix output, and separately
receives the configured target. No parallel Guix package resolver or new release
receipt schema is required. The thirteen-input construction sections below
also describe historical callers; do not revive them as predecessors.

### Requirements and allocation (SRS/SAS/HLD)

The existing CD machine no-regression merge obligation remains. Source admission,
measured package comparison, immutable product admission and target/host receiving
are distinct. Code owns native PNPM selection and exact workspace source reading;
Guix `test-change.mjs` owns the declared engineering comparison; LAGOON owns
immutable application construction and separate host activation/health. This
allocation reuses existing owners instead of introducing another selector,
certificate, lock parser or recipe executor.

Each side must bind its admitted root/configuration/lock and actual native build
to its packed runtime and immutable application output. LAGOON's registered
store output and sealed target/profile/instance/host checks do not independently
attest Git provenance. Its live health check requires the returned artifact to
match the output but belongs to activation, never pre-merge source comparison.
Missing product or read-only target receiving remains incomplete.

### Current registry comparison bindings and custody

The comparison is one retained request with independently selected baseline
and candidate. Each side binds the software repository, exact commit and tree,
the complete mounted-source graph and source-plan digest, every requested owner
head, and that side's target repository/commit/tree/definition. Record the
source-owned consumer coordinate and committed manifest, lock and applicable
PNPM configuration bytes for each selected consumer. A source request is not
an installation receipt. Root-workspace fields from the historical v3 request
cannot stand for the current per-consumer registry inputs. Neither a current
branch tip nor the other side's configuration may fill missing baseline data.

The Code-owned `source-registry-closure` operation supplies its actual
`npm.source-registry-closure.consumers` result; each required package must have
one matching `frozenInstall` row, original consumer path, name/version, manifest
and lock digests, and no refusal. Guix must receive that result from the invoked
owning operation and retain its invocation, selected supplier source, input
identities, logs and output custody. A caller-authored JSON file with these
fields, a prior pin-only preparation or a `node_modules` directory is not proof
that the operation occurred. The complete effective dependency-age policy,
including inherited configuration, must be preserved by that owner. No Guix
override, release exemption or unlocked fallback may repair an unavailable or
too-young dependency. Missing policy-preserving supplier behavior is an explicit
unfulfilled prerequisite, not a passing fixture.

Code's existing `produceRegistryRuntime` consumes the genuine frozen result and
same installed consumer. Retain its `npm.registry-runtime.produced` reading,
manifest/lock/policy digests, root pack integrity, numeric traversal-entry count and confined
runtime output. The returned `files` field is a count, not a byte inventory;
derive any required final runtime inventory by observing the produced tree under
the receiver's retained custody. Match those digests back to the admitted consumer bytes. Its path,
digest and `frozenInstall` checks do not independently authenticate the operation
that produced a supplied receipt; that provenance comes from the retained
owner invocation. If installation custody is transferred, retain the move and
new location; do not pretend the old source directory is still installed.
Neither side may reuse the other's mutable installation or work directory.

Pass that exact portable runtime through the existing LAGOON construction
boundary, retaining the selected constructor/driver/channels identities and
actual registered immutable store output. Read back the result and source
integrity after construction. LAGOON's read-only `check-target` receives each
side's exact configured target/profile/instance/host against that store output;
it does not attest Git-to-runtime provenance. Bind its actual invocation and
reading to the same side and product. Current main must never silently replace
a reviewed target. A pathname or an independently passing target check cannot
repair a missing source/install/pack/product link. Removed or changed owners,
tests and target interfaces retain their baseline obligations.

The existing receiver owns these bindings and final comparison. It may emit
`no-regression` only after both complete chains and all original engineering
obligations have actually been received and final source/store integrity holds.
No new freely supplied success certificate, package resolver or test-command
interface is introduced. Profile changes, retention deletion, activation and
health checks remain outside this pre-merge operation. Reuse the existing runner
and resource admission; a busy lease preserves the same pending operation.

### Required registry-native execution refinement (not yet implemented)

The next implementation unit is the current registry composition in the existing
exact receiver, not another invocation of the historical workspace adapter.
Retain v2/v3 parsing and refusal behavior. The literal request keys and concrete committed consumer-selection carrier still
require refinement against their owning declarations before implementation.
Introduce an explicitly versioned, closed registry request only with its
implementation and original receiver tests;
do not silently reinterpret a v3 recipe/root-lock request as registry input.

#### Source and request admission

The new request must bind the exact controller, independently selected baseline
and candidate software roots, committed consumer-selection declarations, exact
target definitions and requested owner heads. It must not accept caller-selected
test commands, arbitrary package inventories, executable recipes or supplied
success receipts. The existing receiver retains its request digest and original
result carrier; this refinement creates no parallel receiver or certificate.

At `source-pins.mjs` and `materialize-sources.mjs`, reuse
`commonSourceIdentity`, `committedSourceFileIdentity`,
`mountedSourceClosure`, `sourceGraphDelta` and
`nativeTargetSourceIdentity` where their actual contracts apply. Current
registry admission must not call `commonSoftwareSources` merely to manufacture
the retired root workspace. Derive each consumer from its committed declaration
and bind owner, commit, tree, coordinate, manifest, native committed lock and
effective configuration provenance, including absent inputs. Verify requested
heads occur in the candidate composition. Preserve full graph and target deltas.

#### Closed registry source-admission increment

The source-only `guix.reviewed-change-request.v4` has exactly
`kind`, `controller`, `baseline`, `candidate`, and `changes`. Each side has
`software` (repository/commit/tree) and a nonempty bounded `targets` list.
Each target has repository/commit/tree/definition plus `coordinate`, its exact
mounted coordinate in that side's inventory. The target repository's committed
root `package.json` must export `./machine` and `./channels` to that exact
definition's `machine.scm` and `channels.scm`. These existing source declarations
select the deployment consumer; no supplied package list, recipe or test command
is accepted. Duplicate consumer owners refuse.

The increment retains the existing complete source graph, independently verifies
the target's owner/commit/tree/coordinate, and binds the consumer's manifest,
native PNPM lock, local workspace policy and .npmrc presence/absence. It records
whether the receivingTests property is present (including a null value); this
boolean is not validation or enrollment. A committed lock identity does not
prove PNPM validity. Only repository-root consumers, with at most one target
per repository, are supported by this increment. The inventory root
needs no package manifest, workspace or lock. Historical v2/v3 requests retain
their existing meaning and controls.

This is source admission, not the registry execution route. The retained result
is always incomplete with runtimeAcceptance false. The graph tree identities bind
ancestor committed sources, but this increment does not claim a complete effective
policy reading (including inheritance and PNPM hooks), qualified supplier
invocation, original engineering-test coverage,
installation, runtime, store output or target receiving. Those obligations below
remain necessary before this version can return a completed comparison. It does
not admit source-only qualification as a machine merge gate. Requested-head
membership does not establish participation of that change in a built product.

#### One paired operation at the comparison owner

`receiveReviewedChanges` owns request admission and invokes the paired operation
in `test-change.mjs`. For each side, use independent fresh installation and work
custody, qualified immutable suppliers and fixed owner operations:

1. Invoke Code's `receiveSourceRegistryConsumers` for the admitted consumers
   and releases. Retain the real invocation, logs and returned result. Require
   exactly the selected frozen-install rows, no refusal, and matching source,
   manifest, lock and effective-policy identities.
2. Admit original source-declared engineering tests and execute them with the
   existing bounded fixed runner. Do not forge a `readNodeWorkspace` result
   for a root that has no workspace. Use the owning source-reader interfaces
   only within their actual contracts.
3. Pass that actual receiving result and the same installed consumer to
   `produceRegistryRuntime`, with a separate confined destination and
   `transferInstallation: false`. Bind its actual source/output, name/version,
   manifestDigest, lockDigest, workspacePolicyDigest, rootIntegrity and numeric
   files count to the admitted side. Observe any required runtime byte inventory
   directly under retained receiver custody; the API does not return that
   inventory. No caller-provided receipt may replace step 1.
4. Invoke the selected LAGOON driver's existing
   `build CHANNELS LOAD-PATH DEFINITION SOURCE ROOT` boundary. SOURCE is the
   exact produced runtime. Retain the actual registered immutable store output
   and selected driver, definition, channel and Guix identities.
5. Invoke the matching pinned driver's
   `check-target CHANNELS STORE PROFILE TARGET`. Bind its actual output,
   profile, target, main unit, host kind, instance and execution role to the same
   side. This read-only operation does not attest Git-to-runtime provenance.
6. Verify source, configuration and store integrity again before comparison and
   retain both complete source plans in the original receiver result.

The orchestration must not invoke registry publication or LAGOON deploy,
receive or rollback, profile mutation, retention deletion or live health operations. Do not invoke an ordinary
release wrapper whose build-only exit occurs after such effects. Existing lease,
resource and qualified-interpreter admission remain prerequisites.

#### Non-vacuous completion and failure semantics

The existing `changeVerdict` can return no-regression for empty collections.
Therefore clearing `unmeasured` is not an implementation of this requirement.
Before using the existing comparison semantics, require positive, source-bound
completion of every selected consumer, original engineering obligation, runtime,
store product and target check on both sides. Account for removed owners, tests,
enrollment and changed target interfaces. A missing stage or unexplained empty
inventory remains incomplete.

Preserve completed passing/regressing/preexisting outcomes separately from
unavailable measurements and integrity failures. A failed side cannot become an
empty passing side. A stage refusal stops dependent execution for that side,
retains its exact input and cause, and cannot erase the other side's valid
observations. Source or product mutation invalidates dependent observations.
Neither partial success nor a prepared installation establishes machine receiving.

A changed package consumed as a registry dependency must be represented by the
immutable release bytes selected in that side's admitted consumer lock and
resulting product. Testing its source separately while constructing a product
from an older release does not receive that dependency change. Controller,
constructor/driver, channels and target-definition changes instead bind to their
actual qualified invocation or authenticated construction/receiving input and
resulting product; do not invent a registry-publication requirement for such
inputs. Every requested head must have its actual participation established.
Premerge candidate qualification is possible through the existing build/check-target
boundaries; merging suppliers first is not a proof.

#### Acceptance and migration sequence

First qualify closed request/source admission with a real current-style consumer
pair lacking a root workspace and retain all original v2/v3 refusal controls.
Then qualify the invoked Code install/runtime and LAGOON product/target chain.
Finally exercise the original consumer pair and issue the existing bound verdict.
Do not enroll the new request as an executable success route before all required
stages are implemented and independently received.

Required cases include wrong source/tree/coordinate/configuration/lock;
post-execution mutation; fabricated or stale receipts; cross-side installation
reuse; pass/regression/preexisting failure; unavailable measurement; removed
owner/test/target obligation; and genuine policy refusal before dependency
eligibility followed by acceptance with unchanged policy. Independent execution
must receive actual portable runtimes, registered Guix outputs and both target
checks. Effect canaries must establish absence of the excluded operations.
Controlled source cases qualify the implementation, not the real machine.

The publication supplier remains a separately owned prerequisite. At current
`5a85a4f3ef6c2dc7c074d740633507b39b0a8057`, local
`pnpm-workspace.yaml` is staged and bound by `workspacePolicyDigest`; this
supersedes the older observation that only manifest/lock/.npmrc were staged.
Automatic per-release age exclusions and package-local-only policy reading still
do not meet the complete effective-policy contract above. Preserve the newer
supplier work; do not recreate its completed local-policy change or compensate
with a Guix policy override. Keep this exact prerequisite visible while implementing
independent registry request and composition responsibilities at Guix.

### Measurement failures and migration

`runPackageTests` returns its existing file-to-boolean map only for completed
test processes with integer exit status: zero passes and nonzero fails. Launch
errors, timeout/buffer errors, a signal or missing exit status throw
`ERR_TEST_MEASUREMENT_UNAVAILABLE` with the affected file, executable, process
error code, status and signal, retaining completed results separately.
These are unavailable observations, never failed assertions. Existing composed
callers retain their incomplete/prerequisite-failed handling. The package-only
native comparator measures baseline and candidate sequentially and retains each
available side. A side whose admission, execution or final integrity check
fails is omitted and recorded in `receiving.unread` with side/reason; process
measurement errors additionally preserve code/file/executable/process-error-code/
status/signal. Completed rows before an execution failure are retained only as
`receiving.partial[side]`, after the original reader's final integrity check;
they never stand for a complete inventory. Final source verification runs even
after execution fails, and its refusal discards partial rows. It does not
substitute an empty inventory or infer regression, preexisting failure, addition
or retirement from a missing side. The overall verdict is `incomplete`. When
both sides are readable, existing test comparison semantics remain unchanged.
The bounded package path always keeps product/target `unmeasured: true` and
`runtimeAcceptance: false`.

This revision implements those measurement semantics at the existing runner
and comparator. It does not enable registry product execution. Historical v2/v3
requests and their refusals remain unchanged; no request is silently upgraded
and no old root lock is synthesized. Before enabling the current registry path,
refine the existing exact receiver's closed request and source admission to bind
the current consumer inputs, then wire the original Code/LAGOON operations and
their actual custody through that receiver. The historical workspace reader is
not the current registry consumer reader. A merged supplier or an authored
contract alone does not satisfy these implementation obligations.

At the inspected Code publication source
`cd331dad8f5c9336d6ea3eb0d1f2b02ef831d233`, the frozen closure adds release
exclusions and stages only manifest/lock/`.npmrc`; this is not the required
effective-policy guarantee. Correct that existing supplier at its owner before
admitting its result here. LAGOON's read-only target operation is proposed in
`computer-deployment-engine` PR13, head
`1f94535017573c8c6fcb789d7e9d2f3df0372915`; its independent source tests do not
establish accepted supplier installation or either real target's receiving.

Acceptance must distinguish source verification from actual machine receiving:

- Completed passing, candidate-failing and identically failing test processes
  retain pass/regression/preexisting semantics. A launch failure or interrupted
  runner produces an unavailable measurement, never either failure class.
- If either side is unreadable, retain the other side's actual observations,
  identify the unavailable side, and produce incomplete without invented empty
  results. Post-execution source mutation invalidates that side's observations.
- Current registry admission must reject mismatched commit/tree/graph, changed
  manifest/lock/policy bytes, substituted source paths, invented or stale frozen
  receipts, reused installations and missing baseline products before success.
- An actual too-young dependency must refuse under its effective age policy;
  after eligibility the same policy and committed selection must receive through
  the original consumer. Controlled clock/registry fixtures qualify this
  behavior only; they do not install FeltOS or receive its live dependencies.
- Actual runtime packing, registered-store readback and read-only target checks
  must bind both independent sides. Missing product/target measurements or
  retired obligations remain incomplete. No fixture or source test can promote
  that stage to machine `no-regression`, installation or operating acceptance.

### Native package comparison interface (LLD)

`runNativeDeclaredReceiving` consumes the live result of Code's existing
`readNodeWorkspace`, the exact admitted source graph, separate canonical test
work, and the qualified Node interpreter. The underlying original reader has
Git blob `0e09da4df3423d6c5ea8bc0730a7d12e7f842937`, the original package's
accepted sparse-reader refinement at
`node-runtime-release-promotion-service` commit
`f479d7e32debe03ff7d57fee22462c8bca607daa`. It replaces the older reader blob
`2887fa49d5cd3fb5a0afd56aaf4930041acd6dcb` with literal pinned-path lookup,
preserving source identity, confinement and mutation refusal. The integration
keeps an exact-byte guard rather than accepting any package with that name.
On 2026-10-15 Code owner
[`63ba013c6d5e1f5bbb02ecb5dae4a2586f1d65c1`](https://github.com/red-cup-engineering/computer-code-engineering-services-section/commit/63ba013c6d5e1f5bbb02ecb5dae4a2586f1d65c1)
merged the genuine package export
`@red-cup-engineering/node-runtime-release-promotion-service/read-workspace-source`.
Guix already declares this Code package as an original dependency. The native
integration now resolves its published package interface inside the actual
PNPM-installed source workspace, verifies the pinned provider implementation
blob and uses the original function. It no longer admits an arbitrary
`NATIVE_WORKSPACE_READER` file-path supplier or copies the Code implementation.
Real selected dependency-source receiving, native PNPM tests, Code's current
sparse-source proposals, production executor wiring, LAGOON construction and
target activation remain separate gates; an owner-export merge is not their
satisfaction.

The live reader supplies native importer membership, committed byte/provenance
reads and final verification. Caller JSON success flags and arbitrary test
commands are not inputs. Admit every selected manifest/receivingTests declaration
and committed regular test before execution; bind its coordinate and commit to
one admitted graph owner. Reject duplicate importer/name, redirected/outside
paths, graph-root/tree mismatch and cross-owner test declarations. Code includes
administrative root `.` in filtered readings; a root receivingTests declaration
therefore refuses as unmeasured membership rather than silently broadening or
shrinking selection.

Run only the existing `runPackageTests` fixed Node test command, with its bounded
per-file timeout and sanitized environment, in the actual installed package.
Pass absolute admitted test filenames to preserve the existing runner interface;
normalize result keys relative to the package. Leading-dash filenames are files,
never Node options.
Allocate isolated HOME/TMP under the caller's separate work directory. Source
programs remain reviewed trusted code: this is not a hostile-code sandbox.
Call the original reader verification before and after execution; post-run
source mutation refuses. The provider verifies consumed interface bytes and
mount identities, not every possible dynamic import or installed dependency.
Actual frozen installation/toolchain custody is still a caller prerequisite.

Return the existing declared-receiving row shape for measured sides and use unchanged
`compareDeclaredReceiving`/`verdictOf` semantics. The composed
`compareNativeDeclaredReceiving` executes both actual sides, retains baseline and
candidate rows, removed/added/changed tests, and calls `changeVerdict` with target
receiving explicitly unmeasured. Unavailable sides follow the measurement
failure contract above; comparison requires both measured sides. Genuine candidate regressions are reported;
passing packages never become an overall no-regression or runtime acceptance.
The graph is preadmitted by the caller: this adapter checks the root tree and
matched owner commit/coordinate, but does not independently validate non-root
graph trees. Whole-owner/topology accounting via receivingObligations is not
invoked by this package-only path and is not claimed as measured here.
The native-v3 receiver remains source-only; no execution mode is enabled here.

### Verification

Explicit integration suite: `test/native-package-receiving.integration.mjs`.
Supply `NATIVE_PNPM` for PNPM11.28.5 **after** the genuinely
composed native workspace has installed the Guix controller's declared Code
package dependency. The test resolves the Code reader through that package's
public export, verifies its original Git blob and refuses a missing or changed
supplier. It creates actual committed source fixtures, performs native
local/offline installation and a second frozen installation, obtains genuine
PNPM selection, executes original declared Node tests through the comparator,
and checks regression/preexisting/retirement and source-mutation refusals.
There is no caller-supplied reader path or supplied passing-result JSON. This explicit
integration test is outside the default source-only test glob and must be run
when the required genuine provider is available; absence is not a passing run.
Real Guix products and installed target assays remain unexecuted obligations.

## Historical thirteen-input native source admission


The current common-software path is the BCF LAGOON workflow and justfile,
Code's native generation/compilation, PNPM's root frozen installation and
production packing, and the target's `software-release.scm`. The root's native
PNPM11.28.5 selection is not the fixed PNPM10.34.6 provider described below.
Neither that provider receipt nor the retained `release-consumer.json` wrapper
qualifies the current native path.

`receive-pull-requests.mjs --exact` recognizes a closed native v3 request with
independently fixed common baseline/candidate identities and recipe path/blob,
exact targets on both sides, the selected controller and the requested heads.
It reuses the existing source acquisition, recursive graph walker, request
digest and failure receipt. Both roots require their own whole committed root
lock/configuration. Both target sides require nonempty authenticated native
definitions. The complete graph and target deltas preserve introduced, removed
and moved obligations. No caller-provided package list or replacement map
substitutes for either committed graph.

This source-only increment ends with `incomplete`, stage
`native-product-receiving`, and `runtimeAcceptance: false`; it never invokes
`test-change.mjs`, the legacy qualifier, a recipe or candidate program. Native
products and original obligation execution have not been received merely
because source admission passed. All v2 refusal controls remain in place; the
native request does not reinterpret an old request or revive `machine.json`.

## Next native product and obligation boundary

The implementation boundary is the existing receiver and `test-change.mjs`,
with the actual LAGOON construction/collection owner. It is not a second checker
or a new provider certificate. Each independently received side must provide
the actual immutable collection's runtime, BCF ordinary/first-cutover outputs,
CRG ordinary/first-cutover outputs, driver, channels, Guix, root lock, generation
producer, generation plan, emission and compilation. The existing
`software-release.scm` owns their native store validity, single shared runtime
references, source commits, root-lock hash, producer bytes and Code result
contracts. Those thirteen real store inputs must be read back under that side's
authenticated source and the receiver's retained plan, not accepted from an
unverified summary or freely chosen store-path strings.

The actual `NodeRuntimeWorkspaceGenerationPlan`,
`NodeRuntimeWorkspaceEmission` and `NodeRuntimeSourceCompilation` v1 contracts
must remain source-bound. Emission requires unchanged source configuration;
compilation requires the packed-workspace-packages scope and compiled static
bindings; emission and compilation both require `applicationExecuted: false`.
The actual common manifest identifies source-commit, crg-source-commit and
source-lock-sha256. The target pair must be explicitly selected: current main
must not silently replace an older target selected by the source caller.

Native construction must reuse frozen PNPM installation, Code's existing
emission and packed static compilation, and LAGOON's native `build` mode. There
is no lock re-resolution fallback or re-labeling of the old provider. An
existing completed collection may be reused only after the owning store,
source, producer and readback checks. Missing baseline or candidate products
keep receiving incomplete/not-composed.

The same `test-change.mjs` owner must execute the original source-declared
engineering obligations against each side's actual frozen installation, retain
changed-owner attribution, and run each failing candidate obligation against
its exact baseline before calling it preexisting. Removed tests/packages and
retired or changed target interfaces remain baseline obligations. Native target
and host/profile/instance declarations provide the installed public interface
obligations; missing `run.entries` cannot produce an empty passing inventory.
BCF worker/application requirements and CRG actor-only requirements remain
distinct. A final verdict binds both product readings, request/source plan,
original heads and final source/store integrity. Source tests and compilation
alone never prove useful target receiving or operating acceptance.

Execution stays held until that bounded native product/obligation integration
is reviewed. The current ordinary build workflow also updates a common-build
profile and deletes older retention generations before its build-only exit;
the receiver must not execute the whole workflow or release script as its
comparison subprocess. Profile updates, retention deletion, activation and
live receiving are outside this comparison boundary. Reuse the existing
self-hosted Guix runner and lease; this work adds no hosted route or runner.

## Retained fixed legacy provider qualification

This is a source-review-gated CI qualification, not machine source admission.
The existing `test-controller.mjs` admitted-main contract remains unchanged.
The candidate may be tested before merge without claiming that it is an admitted
controller. PR73's held merge and every release acquisition refusal remain held.

## Existing owner and runner

The source owner is `red-cup-engineering/guix-machine-release-service`. A machine's workflow is the controller
bootstrap shape (`scripts/controller-bootstrap.sh`): it names no machine to the controller, and the machine
repository, runner labels and compute lease are the machine's own. The earlier design that projected a
qualification job and a reviewed-change receiving route into a machine's workflow is retired with the workflow
projection module; the change test (`test-change.mjs`) and the exact receiving path
(`receive-pull-requests.mjs --exact`) remain. The old v2 package-workspace
composition contract is distinct from native v3 source admission above; it
does not establish current common-software selection from a target definition.
Private source reads use the same existing Git/gh credential-helper contract;
candidate execution receives an empty environment populated only with fixed
runtime paths, temporary home/directory and locale values.

## Frozen change composition consumes the same provider

The ordinary `test-change.mjs SNAPSHOT WORK OWNER/REPO=PATH ...` interface remains
unchanged. Its `compose` path first runs the existing committed-source preparation,
including current main's strict SQLite source admission. A refused source keeps
the exact repository/commit plan in `WORKSPACE.prepare.json` and full preparation
stdout/stderr beside it for the responsible source owner. Source code and failing
workspace evidence are not removed to make the comparison pass.

Composition requires the snapshot's reviewed dependency lock. There is no lock
generation, resolution retry, workspace `.npmrc`, npm-exec bootstrap or reuse of
the old registry cache. The fixed assay and composition call the same
`realizeToolchain` function, retaining exact installed-channel equality,
`--no-grafts`, all four already-realized prerequisites and the fixed archive
identities. The existing executable home-profile Guix is selected before HOME
isolation, if present; its lexical directory precedes PATH.

The selected toolchain's absolute Node runs the existing
`design-rules.mjs --acquire-guix-frozen` adapter in an explicit minimal
environment. That adapter, unchanged from the qualified provider source,
verifies the complete inspected file inventory and exact Node identity, creates
fresh isolated configuration/cache/store directories, reads the genuine YAML
parser and enforces the existing frozen graph/manifests/configuration checks
before package acquisition. Any failure ends composition; a successful adapter
receipt must name frozen mode and the qualified pnpm version. Recognition of a
version or store-path string alone grants no provider admission.

Head and required base composition use that same selected toolchain. The base is
composed lazily once within the current comparison, only if a failing head test
needs it. Cross-run baseline reuse is disabled because a retained mutable
workspace cannot prove that tests left its source/dependencies unchanged. An old
`.composed` marker therefore never skips admission; it is only local completion
evidence. This costs a fresh baseline composition when needed and avoids adding
a second workspace-integrity framework. TypeScript builds, descriptor generation,
changed package discovery, outside-closure reporting, head/base attribution and
design rule comparison retain their existing behavior.

This adapter binding is independently testable without network acquisition.
The pure/source controls use actual committed-source preparation and closed
subprocess ports for the Guix/provider boundary; they are not a substitute for
the genuine fixed-provider assay or full machine composition. The receiving
report must identify which scope actually ran. A newly encountered source,
manifest or lock refusal requires its exact owning-source correction and a new
reviewed graph, rather than an automatic re-resolution or guard exception.

The release preflight remains closed for build/deploy/check-failure/advance and
early package-cache pruning remains disabled. Its projector recognizes both
fixed-adapter callers only to produce an accurate closed diagnostic. The earlier
version-only legacy-bootstrap refusal remains for callers that still declare
one. No fixed fixture, source test, diagnostic match or provider receipt reopens
production admission, metadata resolution or deployment by itself.

## Actual fixed assay

`qualify-candidate-toolchain.mjs SERVICE SNAPSHOT WORK COMMIT TREE` reuses the
same fixed `qualify-toolchain.mjs` assay as the admitted controller scope.
It checks installed authenticated Guix channels before any time-machine fallback,
requires ungrafted already-realized Node/canonical-tar/canonical-gzip/Guile3.0.11
outputs, realizes
the two official fixed-output pnpm10.34.6/YAML2.9.0 archives, verifies every
inspected package file and actual Node24.18 runtime identity, and runs only
`test/qualification/frozen-acquisition-provider.test.mjs`.
That genuine provider fixture permits only its loopback synthetic package/YAML
archive requests and verifies refusal cases, unchanged frozen lock and worker
boundary initialization. It does not test the production application graph.
Missing installed prerequisites fail; this route does not build a new Node or
install Guix. The unused Node SQLite builtin is not invoked by the assay.

The archive tools are the exact `canonical-package` variants of tar1.35 and
gzip1.14 declared by the authenticated pinned channel. Guix's `%final-inputs`
constructs these bootstrap-final variants, and its own standard patch inputs
use the same selector, keyed by exact package name and version. The fixed assay
is a native host build with no cross-compilation target; cross-building is not
qualified by this contract. They differ from the duplicate top-level package
derivations even though the package names and versions match. The supplier binds
each selected package once and uses that same object for the already-realized
output check and archive extraction. Node retains its existing exact identity.
No grafted, system-PATH, alternate-version or acquisition
fallback is permitted. An absent canonical output still refuses before the
toolchain package can be returned. This selection contract does not itself prove
that the runner currently retains the canonical outputs; the genuine fixed
qualification establishes that and proceeds only if all prerequisites pass.

The build interpreter is the exact channel package `guile-3.0.11`. At the pinned
channel commit, Guix's own self-build selects the identical package through its
`guile-3.0-latest` alias; the generic `default-guile` instead selects the distinct
bootstrap-final Guile3.0.9. The supplier binds `builder-guile` once, checks its
exact ungrafted output with the other prerequisites, and passes that same package
through trivial-build-system's supported `#:guile` argument. Guix threads this
explicit interpreter into module compilation and the final builder. The two
unmodified, unpatched archive origins use the daemon's built-in downloader, which
does not introduce a default-Guile output dependency. No installed executable is
accepted merely because of its name or reported version. Missing exact Guile
still refuses; neither a new Guile build nor fallback to another interpreter is
permitted. Pinned source establishes the package selection, and the genuine
qualification must establish its actual availability and successful use.

The separate receipt is `candidate-toolchain-qualification.json`, kind
`guix-candidate-toolchain-qualification.v1`. It binds candidate commit/tree,
channels and actual toolchain identity. Success and available failure receipts
are copied to the original Actions log before temporary-file cleanup. It cannot
overwrite a full controller-test receipt or claim machine acceptance.

## Root-only validation and execution gates

Before remote publication: syntax-check new JS; run only the fixed pure tests
`test/candidate-toolchain.test.mjs`, plus existing explicitly
selected provider/source refusal controls. These tests must not run Guix or
provider code. 
After owner commit publication, re-read its commit/tree and regenerate the actual
consumer; independently review that exact workflow and candidate before any
dispatch. Retain the existing zero-spend runner and lease. Only the explicit new
manual mode or exact authorized PR comment runs this assay; old release modes remain closed. A failed/missing Guix
prerequisite is a refusal, not permission for fallback installation. Real Guix
execution, Scheme evaluation, relocation, arbitrary graphs and production
activation remain unqualified until their respective genuine evidence exists.

## Fixed controller-source qualification

The independently pinned candidate qualification also enrolls a fixed list of
owner source controls, including every original `test-controller` case, and the
genuine YAML workspace-reading controls. The list lives in
`qualify-controller-sources.mjs`; callers cannot supply a command, file list or
glob. This scope is `fixed-toolchain-and-controller-source`. It does not compose
application repositories or admit a machine release.

The existing toolchain realization, inventory and eighteen provider controls run
first. Their exact Node24.18.0 executable then runs the owner controls. The pinned
installed Guix gate resolves the exact ungrafted `guile-3.0.11` and
`guile-json-4` (4.7.3) outputs used by the pinned Guix self-build. Both must already
be valid store paths. The resolver cannot build, fetch, substitute, time-machine
or use an ambient/version-only interpreter fallback. A missing output is a named
prerequisite failure, not permission to skip a controller case.

The Node package's own existing npm wrapper runs only the controller fixtures'
fixed package-test command with explicit `--offline --no-audit --no-fund
--no-update-notifier --ignore-scripts` CLI flags. These remain effective after
the existing npm environment cleanup, suppress npm's background update query,
and do not skip the declared test body. It does not bootstrap a package manager or install a
dependency. Git, shell and removal utilities are the same existing runner
utilities required by those inert fixtures. The interpreter path precedes
`/usr/bin:/bin`; no ambient HOME, npm configuration, credentials, Node options or
Guile load paths enter this source-test environment. Guile sees only its own core
modules and the exact selected JSON source directory, with auto-compilation off.

The source controls retain their existing inert service/Guix/SSH command canaries
and their real native Scheme predicates. Their expected failures do not become
qualification success unless the test assertion itself passes. The ten genuine
YAML controls independently verify every provider file against its committed
inventory before parsing. Qualification then repeats candidate and channel source
integrity, preserving the existing compute lease and fixed workflow event/source
gates. Receipts identify the interpreter paths and complete enrolled assay list.

Local source-only tests on Node24.19 and absent Guile cannot establish this
receiving result. The exact merged/published candidate must pass these unchanged
controls through the existing reviewed qualification workflow on its already
realized provider; production pins, source acquisition and activation remain
outside this qualification.


## Exact interpreter handoff into change comparison

The fixed receiving procedure retains the successful qualification's exact Node,
Guile and guile-json reading and binds it to the toolchain Node before any source
carriers are fetched. The ordinary comparison receives those same installed
Guile/JSON paths, with auto-compilation disabled, ahead of the trusted workflow
PATH. This keeps the existing Guix command discovery for frozen composition.
Caller GUILE loader variables, credentials and Node loaders are not forwarded.
No new interpreter acquisition, fallback or source-selection authority is added.
Missing, malformed or mismatched prerequisite readings refuse before comparison;
success and failure receipts retain the selected interpreter identities. This
allows the already-enrolled native controller tests to run when its repository
is part of the exact change set; source qualification alone is not a substitute
for those tests or complete machine receiving.

The fixed source list also explicitly includes the owning
`test/typescript-build.test.mjs` six-control assay. It does not discover arbitrary
new tests or accept caller-selected test paths. All prior fixed assays remain.
The exact comparison executes the qualified canonical Node path itself, and
refuses a different interpreter before writing carriers or creating its test home.




## Proposed generic committed release caller

`project-release-caller.mjs` is a whole-workflow source transformer. It replaces
neither the fixed provider nor the exact receiver. It contains no machine catalog,
publication-specific receiver, legacy fragment recognizer or retired projector.
Machine identities and operational choices are explicit consumer-owned input.
The earlier machine-specific workflow projection remains retired.

The consumer commits a JSON input matching `release-consumer-binding.schema.json`
(`guix.release-consumer-binding.v2`). The CLI accepts that committed file and an
optional reviewed fixed-diagnostics JSON file. `readCommittedConsumerBinding`
checks exact Git HEAD blob bytes, regular non-executable data mode and canonical
unredirected path, using read-only Git with global/system configuration, replacement
objects and lazy/network transport disabled. Dirty or hidden working edits refuse.
No consumer code is executed during generation.

`projectCommittedReleaseCaller(file, diagnostics)` records exact source path,
commit/tree/blob and raw-byte SHA-256. The generated workflow embeds the complete
validated binding as canonical JSON plus its SHA-256. `projectReleaseCaller`
is the underlying pure source transformation used by fixtures and proposal tools;
it does not certify that an arbitrary in-memory object was committed. Production
source handoff uses the committed-input API and normal immutable source review.
At runtime the emitted workflow is checked against its exact trusted workflow
commit before authority is used; no mutable dispatch field selects a binding.
Generation is not a dispatch, build, deployment or runtime admission.

The closed schema carries repository and numeric ID, canonical definition and
fixed workflow path, actor, exact channel blob, existing runner labels, lease,
concurrency, run directory, events, modes and source policy. Unknown keys,
expressions, shell fragments, unsupported events/modes and path traversal refuse.
The supported execution platform is an explicitly named existing self-hosted
Linux/X64/Guix runner; safe additional labels are consumer data. Lease/run paths
are canonical home-relative cache paths. Default mode is always build, and
activationHeld requires build-only modes. Non-build modes require explicit
trusted manual dispatch; diagnostics cannot inherit ordinary mode selection.

The required effects object consists only of typed choices for owner-defined
operations: deinitializeSubmodules, prepareBuildSwap, queuedHeadCheck,
rebuildOutcomeCache, and nullable cpuWeight {unit,weight}. Unit syntax and the
1–10000 weight range are closed. No consumer supplies a command, executable,
script, schedule, advancement route or arbitrary operation name. The one-way SQL
text refusal remains an explicit sourcePolicy over committed declarations; a
negative text scan is never provider or resolved-source qualification.

Diagnostic options are qualification {snapshot,controller}, receiving (the
existing strict v2 request), and optional fixed pullRequestHeadRef. Both machine
identities bind the consumer repository and canonical definition. Candidate roots
remain separate, full graph authority stays in the receiver, and source reading
is separated from credential-free candidate execution. Distinct diagnostic queues
share the explicit existing lease. The authenticated event bytes, fixed-base-descendant PR
carrier, whole-tree workflow-only change restriction and source/effect refusal
controls remain. No old YAML or fragment is accepted as caller authority.

Ordinary bootstrap and diagnostics emit the same filter-free verifier outside
selected checkouts. The shared synchronous core checks committed bytes/modes,
exact stage0 index and filesystem inventory without Git status. Root .git and
committed inert machine Gitlinks are excluded; nested .git and untracked/ignored
files or symlinks refuse. Empty retired directories remain compatible. Exact
verified cache reuse skips fetch/checkout. Fresh acquisition uses the isolated
existing reader context, preserving explicit gh inputs and excluding ambient Git
configuration; selected code does not certify itself. Failed gates clear stale
controller paths and exit independently of caller errexit.

Every generated run body is rejected above 21,000 characters. Tests retain the
longest allowed review branch and paired roots, genuine cache/filter/hidden-edit
refusals, and exact shell/metadata effects. The unchanged current
controller-names-no-machine test remains authoritative. Consumer reference
fixtures document actual policy consequences without becoming an owner catalog;
see release-caller-policy-comparison.md and release-caller-test-migration.md.
Supplemental local YAML parsing remains distinct from installed provider or
whole-machine runtime receiving.

<!-- End owning generic committed release caller contract. Shared graph,
preparation, receiving and transport law may be appended below this boundary. -->

## One exact source graph and two comparison sides

`source-pins.mjs` owns the shared traversal. Ordinary `machineSources` keeps its
recursive mounted-source reading and its explicit injectable mount-reader API.
Declaration-only readings have no graph digest and no fabricated tree identity;
passing one directly as preparation authority refuses. Exact materialization,
comparison and receiving acquire committed objects through their own source-reader
ports, then use that same traversal with actual commit/tree identities. Offline
readers never obtain missing objects through an implicit promisor fetch.

The graph retains every authored origin edge before immutable owner deduplication,
including its authored commit, selected commit and tracked ref. Conflicting pins,
refs, malformed metadata, cycles and unused requested replacements refuse. Every
root-to-owner coordinate is propagated after cycle checks, including descendants
of multiply reached owners. The existing history/data-record exclusion evaluates
all those coordinates. One eligible coordinate admits one immutable owner; all
excluded coordinates remain evidence, and unrelated executable sources cannot
revive an archived owner. Changed-package attribution uses the same predicate.
A same-commit coordinate change remains in the graph delta and can introduce or
retire executable package ownership.

Root repository identity and its canonical definition path are separate. Both
comparison sides authenticate their complete source roots, selected definitions,
locks and channels. Explicit candidate-root changes and parent-introduced topology
are comparison inputs, not pin updates. Requested child substitutions are allowed
only against the resolved baseline or an already authored candidate edge; a third
pin refuses. Ordinary release never receives a replacement map.

## Generic mounted receiving and preparation

Accepted owner changes #110 and #111 remain governing: enrolled receiving packages
receive their own genuine committed mounted children in place, with no package,
machine or publication-name catalog. This materializes all regular committed
mounted bytes inside the receiving package. The retired publication-selector
protocol and its filtered-content assay are historical evidence only.

`prepare-sources.mjs` re-derives any supplied exact side graph from the actual
committed carriers before preparing package bytes. Ordinary preparation requires
authored and selected pins to agree. Comparative preparation carries a typed
`guix.comparison-preparation.v1` value with the complete graph pair, plan, digest
and side. Its edges must match the actual authored parent links and exact selected
child carriers. Generic mount receipts retain both commits when they differ.
The exact receiver independently binds the plan to the reviewed immutable request;
a self-claimed hash is not external authorization.

Prepared runtime entries and receiving enrollment must equal the authored side's
graph. Ordinary build entries also remain exact. Comparison may add unique build
entries only from the authored machine closure, retaining the original build
entries. A declaration-only reading, dropped receiving suite, unrelated entry,
altered graph, unknown third pin or unmatched selected child refuses. Comparative
preparation metadata retains its side and plan identity and does not claim runtime
acceptance.

Changed-package tests and genuine manifest-declared receiving tests remain
separately attributed. Baseline enrollment, removed declared test paths, retired
entries and formerly executable packages stay explicit obligations. An empty
changed-package list cannot erase them or convert missing receiving into a pass.
Each side uses its selected interpreter and channel policy; both are measured by
the same candidate wiring reader under the existing design-rule ratchet. Missing
Guile, frozen provider or application closure remains an actual failed prerequisite.

## Exact request and source artifact transport

`guix.reviewed-change-request.v2` binds baseline root/definition, exact controller,
unique requested owner changes and an optional separately authenticated candidate
root. Fixed JSON bytes and digest precede source/provider execution. Returned
results must bind the entire `guix.comparison-source-plan.v1`, requested identities,
coordinate-aware graph delta and both graph digests. After execution the controller,
machine roots and source carriers are verified again.

The existing full-source export/verify/restore APIs use
`guix.change-source-carriers.v2` and `guix.exported-comparison-inputs.v2`. They
carry independent local Git object stores for both graph sides, exact inventories
and size/file bounds, no borrowed alternates, hardlinks, mutable checkout authority,
reader credentials or implicit transport. Restoration re-derives the same graph
pair before handing it to the existing `test-change` runner. No parallel test
framework or activation route is introduced.

Continuous Delivery PR13 remains an external adoption obligation: its currently
pinned helper admits v1, restores one root and validates only requested replacements.
Its worker, complete-root preparation and report consumer must migrate together to
v2 with an actual immutable helper/controller identity. Genuine hosted runtime/image
qualification remains required; this source proposal creates no runtime binding,
credential grant, machine pin or deployment.


## Composable diagnostic caller and committed declaration

`projectReleaseDiagnosticJobs(binding, diagnostics)` is the closed diagnostic
component used by the complete `projectReleaseCaller` generator. It applies the
same validated consumer schema, fixed snapshot/controller identities and exact
receiving-request contract before emitting any jobs. Its result contains only
job bytes, their event conditions, concurrency selectors, dispatch modes and the
explicit pull-request requirement. It accepts no ordinary commands or arbitrary
workflow input. `projectCommittedReleaseDiagnosticJobs` first authenticates the
actual committed consumer input and returns its exact blob/raw-byte provenance.

A consumer may preserve an existing ordinary workflow while replacing its
obsolete diagnostic job through this component. Such composition must separately
review the surrounding event triggers, mode choices, workflow/job concurrency,
actor/repository gates and ordinary-job exclusions. The fragment itself does not
prove those surrounding obligations. Both routes retain the same compute lease,
fixed source/channel/workflow gates and credential-free executor; diagnostic
selection cannot choose release, promotion or deployment effects.

A machine definition may optionally include the exact `release-consumer.json`
basename as a regular nonexecutable committed declaration file. This applies at
the repository root and at a nested definition. It does not allow sibling JSON
names, a nested substitute path or application code. Actual bytes/mode must match
the committed blob. This filesystem allowance does not admit the declaration's
policy: `readCommittedConsumerBinding` and `releaseConsumerBinding` must still
validate its full closed schema before projection. The artifact is included in
the definition's immutable source identity when present.

A fixed-controller toolchain diagnostic uses its authenticated machine snapshot
for channel/definition data. It need not select or build the machine's full
application graph, and its success is not application or retained-state receiving.
The actual installed Guix/Guile/channel prerequisites remain observable; an
unavailable installed prerequisite is never replaced by a fabricated qualification.


### Fixed diagnostic consumer input under the producer owner

`specifications/release-consumer-cold-red-gate-diagnostic.json` is an explicit
reviewed input for the existing Cold Red Gate diagnostic component. Callers
select this committed file by its exact source path and immutable provider
commit; there is no machine-name lookup, automatic discovery or policy catalog
in executable code. `readCommittedConsumerBinding` authenticates its actual
commit, tree, blob and raw bytes before the same closed schema is applied.
Its declared target repository is separate from the repository carrying the
reviewed generator input. Record both identities when publishing a projection.

The input governs the diagnostic component only. It is not a statement that
Cold Red Gate's legacy ordinary workflow has adopted the build-only held policy
that a whole-caller generation from this binding would produce. Preserving an
existing ordinary job requires its separate source/effect review.

The current ordinary controller34277 does not admit a new consumer declaration
file in its machine definition. The diagnostic maintenance therefore projects
from this actual producer-owned specification input and adds no machine file or
allowlist exception. It does not advance the ordinary controller or disguise
caller metadata as deployment data. The optional machine-local declaration law
remains available to a consumer that actually selects the supporting successor.


## Reviewed PR carrier for fixed qualification

The existing `pullRequestHeadRef` option also permits a qualification-only
component. Its base is the fixed `qualification.snapshot.commit`; the controller,
snapshot tree, definition and channel identity remain independently fixed. This
is the same reviewed workflow-only PR carrier used by exact receiving, not a
general branch execution or dispatch API. When an exact receiving request is
also present, that request retains the PR event and qualification retains its
existing manual/comment semantics. Empty diagnostic input still refuses.

The event and carrier gates retain exact actor, triggering
actor, sender and PR author; same repository and numeric identity; open nonfork
review branch; exact main base; verified base-ancestor workflow-only head; exact synthetic
merge parents; committed regular workflow bytes; and repeated event/source
identity checks before source acquisition or selected execution. Only opened,
synchronize and reopened PR events may select it. A changing base or extra source,
lock or metadata refuses rather than adopting new inputs.

A reviewed PR may retain ordinary append-only history, including merge commits.
The fixed base must be an actual ancestor of its admitted head; immediate-parent
equality is not required. Git ancestry reads disable replacement objects, grafts
and commit-graph shortcuts. Exact synthetic merge parents remain `[base, head]`.
The complete final base-to-head delta must still be the regular workflow only;
intermediate superseded source is neither selected nor executed.

For a shallow PR checkout, after exact event admission the existing source reader
unshallows only the immutable head from digest-checked event bytes, with no tags.
This explicitly acquires its history, including reachable historical file objects;
it is not a metadata-only or zero-acquisition claim. Missing ancestry refuses.
The event digest is checked again after acquisition and before carrier/byte gates,
lease acquisition or any fixed controller import. Ordinary and manual routes are
unchanged. No forced ref update, backup branch or rewritten PR history is required.

Whole-caller generation gives this component its existing independent diagnostic
queue and excludes its condition from ordinary release. A component consumer
must preserve those surrounding conditions explicitly. Its stricter in-job
refusal never enables a fallback ordinary job. Unrelated PRs and existing manual
main semantics retain their ordinary policy. The existing shared compute lease,
fixed installed-channel/toolchain gates and credential-free qualification remain;
no source advance, application assembly, deployment or automatic main merge is
introduced. Publishing an admitted PR carrier can run genuine native qualification
through the existing workflow and therefore requires exact source/event review
before publication, rather than treating it as an inert source proposal.
